module main

# spec-v0.md §7 rule 9 permits ONE effect variable per signature. Every signature
# here quantifies exactly one (`e`), so every one of them is conformant.
#
# The twist that made this a false rejection: `maybe_do`'s `!{e}` is *unpinned* —
# it appears only in the return effect, with no parameter to constrain it. Each
# call instantiates it fresh, so calling it twice in one body merges `$e0` with
# `$e1` into a two-label row, and a rule-9 check that read the INFERRED effect
# saw that row and reported "combines two effect variables". The suggested fix —
# write the same variable on every parameter — was already true of the source.
#
# The accept twin `effect_one_variable_ok.spr` cannot catch this: there the
# variable is pinned by a higher-order parameter, so the two instantiations
# unify and collapse before the merge ever sees a row.
#
# Rule 9 is a statement about the SIGNATURE, so the check counts the effect
# variables the declaration names, not the shape inference happens to build.

fn maybe_do(n: Int) -> Unit !{e} = ()

fn caller(n: Int) -> Unit !{e} =
  do
    maybe_do(n)
    maybe_do(n)

fn main() -> Unit !{IO} =
  do
    caller(1)
    print("ok")
